v1.5
  A GX device with nothing on it can now be set up from a USB stick, without a
  console, an SSH session or anything typed. Venus OS unpacks archives it finds
  on removable media at boot and SetupHelper's Package manager installs package
  archives lying beside them, so a stick carrying SetupHelper's installer, this
  package and an empty AUTO_INSTALL_PACKAGES file is a complete install.

  tools/make-usb-zip.sh builds that stick, and a workflow republishes it on
  every change under a rolling tag, so the download link in the ReadMe always
  carries the current version. SetupHelper's own installer is not in the zip -
  its repository carries no licence - and the note inside the zip says where to
  get it. Together with the adoption path from v1.4 the whole chain needs no
  laptop: stick in, reboot, plug in a bare Arduino, and the fan appears.

  The package now ships an empty AUTO_INSTALL file, which SetupHelper reads as
  "install a new version of this package as soon as one arrives", whether or
  not automatic installs are enabled system-wide. It speaks only for this
  package; whether new versions are fetched at all stays the system-wide
  GitHubAutoDownload setting, and the ReadMe now documents its modes.

  Documentation only otherwise - the driver, the sketch and the firmware are
  unchanged.

v1.4
  A bare Arduino can now be brought into service without a laptop. Until now
  the first board on a GX device was a chicken and egg: nothing identified
  itself, so no service appeared, so there was no button to press, so the
  sketch had to come from the Arduino IDE.

  On an installation that has never had a transmitter, and only there, the
  driver now flashes the one AVR bootloader it finds among the candidate
  ports. Exactly one: two AVRs and it keeps its hands off rather than
  overwrite somebody's other project, and a port that is remembered - even a
  port that has since disappeared - means this is not a fresh installation
  and nothing is adopted. It is attempted once per installation, recorded in
  /Settings/Devices/maxxfan/AdoptAttempted, so a board that cannot be flashed
  is not reset every time daemontools restarts the service.

  A port named on the command line is now flashed if it is bare, for the same
  reason the remembered port is: naming it says whose board it is.

  Fixed: the repair path asked the bootloader for its signature after probe()
  had already handed the port back to serial-starter, so a Venus driver could
  be holding it during the flash. Both flashing paths now take the port for
  the attempt and release it only if nothing of ours is there.

  Driver only - the sketch and arduino/maxxfan_tx.hex are unchanged at 1.3.

v1.3
  The transmitter can be flashed from the GX device. Venus OS has no avrdude,
  but the Arduino bootloader speaks STK500v1, and tools/flash.py implements
  enough of it over pyserial - so once the Arduino is plugged into the GX it
  never has to go back to a laptop.

  The sketch now answers "MAXXFAN <protocol> <version>", the driver publishes
  that version on the device page, and a new element in the bottom right of the
  card shows both firmware versions and flashes the Arduino when pressed:

      Transmitter 1.2 (update to 1.3)

  It is called Transmitter because the card sorts its controls by label, which
  puts it after Speed and clear of the fan controls.

  Flashing happens on the button, never by itself, with exactly one exception:
  if the port that identified as ours last time now has a bootloader but no
  working sketch - an interrupted update - the driver finishes the job at
  startup. Anywhere else a bootloader is not enough, because it only says an
  AVR lives here, not whose project it runs. The bootloader cannot overwrite
  itself, so a failed write leaves a broken sketch and a working bootloader,
  which is what that repair path is for.

  arduino/maxxfan_tx.hex ships with the package. tools/build-hex.sh rebuilds it
  and its version file from SKETCH_VERSION, and tools/check-hex.py - run by a
  GitHub workflow on every change - verifies that they agree. It reads the
  version string out of the committed .hex, where the sketch's identify reply
  puts it, rather than comparing against a fresh build: two toolchain versions
  produce different binaries from identical source, so a byte comparison fails
  for reasons that say nothing about whether the firmware is current.

  Also new: tools/test-flash.py drives the programmer against a simulated
  STK500v1 bootloader on a pty (16 checks: hex parsing, checksums, page
  writing, verification catching a bad page, wrong signature, silent port,
  closed sync window), and tools/test-sketch/ runs the sketch's serial parsing
  on any machine without an Arduino.

v1.2
  A code review of v1.1 found one problem that could damage an unrelated part of
  the system, and a number of smaller ones. Nothing here changes how the fan is
  controlled; it changes what happens when something goes wrong.

  The service used to hand every candidate port to stop-tty.sh before the driver
  knew which one was the Arduino, and never called start-tty.sh. Those are chip
  names, not device names: Victron's RS485-to-USB interface - the cable to a
  Carlo Gavazzi grid meter - is a plain FTDI FT232R and matched the same glob.
  On such a system the meter lost its driver at every start of this service, did
  not come back, and with no Arduino present the whole thing repeated every
  twelve seconds. The release now happens in the driver, for one port at a time,
  immediately around the identification, and a port that does not answer is
  handed straight back. The port that did answer is remembered, so from the
  second start onwards no foreign port is touched at all. The ReadMe describes
  the udev rule that avoids the whole dance.

  A value the driver refused was stored and published anyway. The callbacks
  returned True, which vedbus takes as "accepted" and which makes it store the
  caller's value - so a stepped switch sending step numbers left the display
  showing 3 % while the fan ran at 50. All the guards now return False.

  A dead serial link stayed dead. A USB re-enumeration leaves the by-id path in
  place and the file descriptor unusable, and the driver only exited when the
  path was gone. It now also exits when the port moved to a different tty or
  when several transmits in a row failed, so daemontools can start it again.

  A failed transmit is retried three times instead of being dropped silently,
  because the state was already stored and shown before it went out.

  Resend transmits the shadow state instead of asking the sketch to repeat its
  last packet - after a GX reboot the sketch has none, so the button failed in
  exactly the situation it exists for, and marked the device disconnected.

  Also: outputs configured as a toggle no longer spring back to on, because
  /State is only forced for the types that carry their value in /Dimming; a type
  outside ValidTypes is refused; CustomName, Group and ShowUIControl are stored
  instead of silently reverting at the next start; a stored speed the fan cannot
  do is snapped into range instead of blocking every transmit; /State stops
  reporting Connected while /Connected is 0; /HardwareVersion is published.

  The sketch discarded only one character of an over-long line and then executed
  the rest as a command - 64 characters of padding followed by a command would
  run that command. The whole line is dropped now and answered with ERR. Numbers
  are parsed with strtol: atoi is 16 bit on AVR, so 65636 arrived as 100 and
  passed every range check. A bare CR terminates a line as well, so a client
  with CR endings no longer waits forever, and the warn bit is cleared after
  transmitting so R does not beep a second time.

  find-port.sh no longer lists an FTDI twice - it matched both the FTDI and the
  FT232 pattern. The documented test one-liner takes the port the driver
  identified rather than the first candidate.

  ReadMe: the claim that the port search never matches Victron's own cables was
  wrong and is corrected; the setpoint statistic said 20 of 38 and is 18 of 40;
  the service name is marked as an example, since it follows the tty; the manual
  installation route needs SetupHelper too. The screenshot is 45 kB instead of
  237 kB, because SetupHelper copies it onto every GX device.

  New: tools/test-driver-logic.py, which runs the D-Bus behaviour against a fake
  service with vedbus's real accept/reject semantics, without a GX device.

v1.1
  the speed control is a basic slider now, not a stepped switch. The stepped
  switch turned out to send the number of the position it sits on - 1 to 7 -
  and to ignore the minimum, maximum and step size the driver publishes, so
  every position arrived as a single digit and the fan stayed at 10 %. The
  stepped switch is no longer offered for the speed, and a value that is not a
  valid percentage is refused with a line in the log rather than rounded into
  something the user did not ask for.

  Verified on Venus OS v3.79 against a MaxxFan Deluxe: fan, speed, direction,
  lid, mode and both buttons all behave, and the fan follows the slider.

v1.0
  first release. Publishes the MaxxFan on the Venus OS D-Bus as
  com.victronenergy.switch, with a card in the switch pane holding fan on/off,
  speed, direction, lid, manual/auto, the thermostat setpoint and two buttons
  for re-sending the state and for making the fan beep. The state is kept in
  the Venus settings and survives restarts.

  The Arduino sketch in arduino/ computes the remote control packet from the
  requested state rather than replaying recorded pulse trains, which is what
  makes the setpoint reachable at all. Its 38 kHz carrier comes from timer 1 in
  hardware, so the serial port keeps working while a packet is transmitted -
  earlier sketches disable interrupts for the whole 150 ms and lose commands
  that arrive in that window.

  Two findings from checking the encoder against 99 signals captured from an
  original remote, both of which differ from what the published ESPHome
  component does:
    - the symbol period is 834 us, not 800 us
    - the setpoint is stored in Fahrenheit and the conversion from the Celsius
      the remote displays truncates towards zero, so trunc(C * 1.8) + 32. A
      rounded conversion is off by one degree on 20 of the 38 setpoints.
  tools/verify-encoder.py re-runs that comparison.
